\documentclass{article}
\title{Perception Benchmarks for Urban Autonomous Driving}
\author{O. Farrell and K. Taniguchi}
\date{March 2026}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\section{Introduction}
Reliable perception remains the bottleneck for urban autonomous driving.
We benchmark three detector families on a new night-time dataset
and report detection quality separately for each weather condition.

\section{Related Work}
Public driving benchmarks have driven rapid progress in daytime perception,
but their night-time slices are usually too small for reliable conclusions.
Recent synthetic data approaches narrow the gap,
though the sim-to-real transfer remains imperfect in adverse weather.

\section{Dataset}
The dataset contains forty thousand annotated frames
collected over six months of fleet operation in two cities.
Annotations cover vehicles, pedestrians, cyclists, and traffic signals.
Every frame is labelled with the weather and illumination condition
recorded by the vehicle at capture time.

\section{Metrics}
We report mean average precision at the standard overlap threshold.
For safety critical classes we also report recall at a fixed false positive rate,
since missed pedestrians matter far more than spurious detections.

\section{Results}
Detection quality degrades sharply in rain and at night.
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\hline
Condition & mAP \\
\hline
Clear day & 0.91 \\
Overcast day & 0.88 \\
Night, dry & 0.79 \\
Night, rain & 0.71 & 0.66 \\
Fog & 0.63 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
The gap between clear day and night rain exceeds twenty points
for every detector family we evaluated.

\section{Analysis}
Sensor fusion with radar closes roughly half of the gap,
but lidar dropout remains the dominant failure mode in heavy rain.
Temporal aggregation helps in fog,
where single frames often lack usable structure.
Ensembling the three detector families adds a further point
at the cost of doubling the inference budget.

\section{Limitations}
The dataset covers two cities in a single climate zone,
and the labelling of distant pedestrians is noisy at night.
Results should not be extrapolated to snow or dust conditions.

\section{Conclusion}
Night-time rain is still far from solved for camera based stacks.
We release the dataset and the evaluation code to support reproducible progress.
\end{document}

